					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/accelerando" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1599414); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1599414&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605861457" alt="Accelerando" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1599414)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/accelerando" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1599414); return false;" >Accelerando</a></span> <span class="odate time_1495649959 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 May 2017 18:19</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2831383">
									

<p>A longer article is usually more challenging to present, since keeping a reader engaged throughout it is tough. When the author manages a great ending, it tends to get an overall better reception, for immersion and essentially telling a story.</p>
<p>Presenting a shorter article is riskier, because the story-telling is left to the reader once they're given the info. It could</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-1584">An older example of a short article that manages to quickly engage a reader with an unusual idea.</a> The last addendum seals up the background nicely.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-1468">An example of another animal that carved into wood, longer than the current one but with more context to portray it as tragic.</a> Empathy is created, while there are many open-ended questions still remaining.</li>
</ul>
<p>I think what makes this article less remarkable is, while it has some historical context, the anomaly itself is beetles with tribal behavior. Because most readers don't know much about the Celtics and France, they aren't left questioning why this is happening, when SCP-1468 is clearer about the weirdness of the situation.</p>

							</div>

								
					
