					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2156983&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605861457" alt="psul" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2156983)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;" >psul</a></span> <span class="odate time_1495716388 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 May 2017 12:46</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2832311">
									

<p>This is very close to an upvote from me - the overall image was quite good, and I didn't feel the need for greater explication or backstory. What undermines it is the writing, which is sometimes a touch inexpertly phrased.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It appears to be a shrine, with a roof held up by four pillars, which has a basin on a pedestal in the middle.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This scans oddly to me - seemingly trying to fit slightly too many ideas in the one sentence (perhaps because of the comma placement, or the use of &quot;which has&quot; instead of &quot;and&quot;). There are a few examples of this construction that read similarly oddly - the first sentence of the Description is another.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>all died of heavy trauma or resulting blood loss</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure that &quot;heavy trauma&quot; is sufficiently precise, and you clarify later that in two cases it was head trauma. Generally the addendum shows signs of having been written &quot;on the hoof&quot;, and could do with some tightening.</p>

							</div>

								
					
